WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.567
-Our next guests
are comedians and writers

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.767
whom you know from their work
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:07.334
which airs this Saturday
with host Timothee Chalamet

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:09.701
and musical guests Boygenius.
They wrote and starred

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:12.701
in "Please Don't Destroy: The
Treasure of Foggy Mountain,"

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:15.000
which is streaming
on Peacock November 17th.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.033
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.167
[ Organ playing ]

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:22.267
-Whoo! Yay, baby! You got this.
You're so good.

00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:24.000
-Go, Mart!
-Thank you guys

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.167
for coming, by the way.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:26.667
I know it means a lot to him
that you're here.

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:28.734
-Amy, of course.

00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:30.367
What the [bleep] is her deal,
man?

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.234
-Shut up.
She's right next to us.

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:34.767
-What is her problem?
-She's fine, man. She's --

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.534
She's honestly really nice.
It's just...

00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:37.601
I don't know why
Martin is so --

00:00:37.601 --> 00:00:39.734
-Afraid?
He's petrified right now.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.300
-It looks like
he's being held at gunpoint.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.734
-I know, and what the [bleep] is
going on with that suit?

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:45.300
-I don't know, man.
-It's huge, dude.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.868
He looks like the [bleep] sun.

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:48.934
-He looks like a pimp
in a high school play.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:52.133
-I baptize thee in the name
of the Father,

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.601
the Son and the Holy Ghost.

00:00:55.601 --> 00:00:58.634
You are now baptized.
-Whoo!

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:02.400
That was so good.
[ Applause ]

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.868
-Please welcome to the show
from the comedy group

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:07.000
Please Don't Destroy,
Martin Herlihy,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.701
John Higgins, and Ben Marshall.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:20.033
♪♪

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:30.033
♪♪

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:32.067
Welcome, gentlemen.
-Hi!

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:34.767
-I'm so happy to have you
and such nice timing.

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:36.868
I also though
want to compliment you.

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:38.434
You were going to come
no matter what.

00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:40.100
We just weren't going to talk
about the movie.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.701
And as someone with a show,
I appreciated

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.133
that you were not
going to leave us scrambling.

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:45.167
So thank you.
-We love Seth Meyers.

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:46.300
-Oh, that's very kind.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:50.701
And I been a huge fan of yours
from even before "SNL."

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:54.601
We were actually talking about
working on a project together.

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:55.801
I mean, you guys
are still young,

00:01:55.801 --> 00:01:57.234
but I felt like you
were even younger then.

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:59.634
That's how time flies.
-Yeah, I remember

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.133
we were at dinner with you
when we got an offer

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:04.133
to play our first college.
-Yeah.

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:06.501
-At Iowa State University.
-Iowa State. Yeah.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:09.000
-And we were like, "This is
going to change our lives."

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.367
-It was the best
because you guys were so happy.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:13.767
I remember,
I think John got the e-mail

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:15.200
and we were in the middle
of dinner and you're like,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.767
"Guys, drop everything."
-"Dude."

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:20.801
-"We're going to Iowa!"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.801 --> 00:02:22.501
-At dinner with Seth Meyers.
It's like,

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:26.501
"Shut up, shut up. Iowa State."

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:27.767
-In their main arena?

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:30.567
No, it's like
a smaller theater in the...

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:33.167
-It's going to be
about half full.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:34.801
-But I remember -- I don't know
if you guys remember.

00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:37.534
I was like, "Oh, try
to not forget. This is awesome."

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:39.234
Like as happy
as you are today.

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:41.667
It's a hard thing to hold on to.

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:42.834
But you guys were --
-Much happier then.

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:44.234
-Yeah. Much happier then, right.

00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:46.701
Because now you're on "SNL"
and you have to fail every week.

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:47.934
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.868
-Or the potential of failure
is very real.

00:02:49.868 --> 00:02:52.467
This is the third season
for you guys.

00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:55.033
Certainly from watching
on the show,

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.834
you guys have an incredible
success rate with your shorts.

00:02:57.834 --> 00:03:00.200
But does it feel
more at home now?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.100
-Maybe.
-Yeah.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.033
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.400
We do.
-Were you going to say no?

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.934
-I was going to say
maybe next year but I...

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:10.968
-I feel more at home in that,
like, this is our home

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:13.234
because we, like, live here now.
-Yes, yes.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:17.501
-We used to stay here until
like 6 a.m. two nights ago.

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:19.534
-In the sense
that home is where you sleep.

00:03:19.534 --> 00:03:22.033
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:25.067
Who among you, like,
for example, Bad Bunny,

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:26.601
you did
a wonderful sketch with him

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:29.067
where he was dressed like Shrek

00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:31.534
and he was pitching you guys
a Shrek sketch.

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:34.467
So I'm going to guess
that one came up late at night.

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:35.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.868 --> 00:03:39.934
And like, who among you has
the first seed of this idea?

00:03:39.934 --> 00:03:41.734
-That was Marty.
-Marty had that.

00:03:41.734 --> 00:03:43.868
-And how does it come
to you, Martin?

00:03:43.868 --> 00:03:47.667
-I think it was
a panic sort of situation.

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:49.000
-Yeah.
-That one.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.467
But that one, we wrote it.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:53.501
We like maybe started it
at like midnight or 1:00,

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:56.400
and then we added some stuff
throughout the course

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.167
of the week,
and then the final edit

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:00.467
is exactly what we wrote
at midnight.

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:02.634
-Oh, see, that happened a lot.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:04.734
Sometimes you would write
something,

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:07.267
it would get picked
and then you overwrite it.

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:08.634
-Yes.
-And then you realize,

00:04:08.634 --> 00:04:11.300
oh, weirdly, the crazy idea
we had was the best idea.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.467
I will say to your credit,
Martin, that is a funny idea.

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:15.067
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.067 --> 00:04:16.434
Were you worried
when you came up with it?

00:04:16.434 --> 00:04:19.100
"Oh, I bet a lot of other people
are writing him as Shrek."

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.534 --> 00:04:22.601
-It's so top of mind
right now.

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:24.267
-It is.
When you see Bad Bunny,

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.567
you're like, what if?

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:29.767
Alright, so the movie,
you guys want to set up

00:04:29.767 --> 00:04:31.300
the premise
of the film real quick?

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.067
-Yes.
The movie, it's crazy at "SNL"

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:36.934
because we just play like Ben,
John and Martin,

00:04:36.934 --> 00:04:39.000
these like friends
who work together.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.767
So in this movie, we got to play
Ben, John and Martin,

00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:43.300
who work together.
-Yeah.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.467
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:46.300
For me, it was thrilling
to see the departure.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.634
-Yeah, it's nice.

00:04:47.634 --> 00:04:50.434
-We have so much range.
-Yeah.

00:04:50.434 --> 00:04:53.200
-Did you ever consider
giving yourself different names?

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.534
-No.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:56.100
-We thought about it
for a second,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.534
but I couldn't imagine being
like, "Steve, don't do that."

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:00.667
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.834
-"Shut up, Eric!"
-"Eric!"

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:04.100
-We both went with Eric.
-Yeah.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.601
-We did.
Yeah, but the movie,

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:09.467
it's about three losers
named Ben, Martin, and John.

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:10.834
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:10.834 --> 00:05:14.601
-That's not funny.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.601 --> 00:05:18.534
Who, like, are
feeling stuck in their lives.

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:21.567
And they think the way to change
that is to go out

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:24.767
and find treasure
on their local mountain.

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:27.033
Kind of like "Goonies"
for 27-year-olds.

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:28.367
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:30.734
It has a very nice --
I mean this in a genuine way.

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:33.167
It has a very good "Goonies"
vibe.

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:34.701
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:37.267
-You told us that you
watched it on your phone, right?

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:39.567
-I watched your movie
on my phone, yes.

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:42.534
And the last time I watched
"Goonies" was on an airplane.

00:05:42.534 --> 00:05:44.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.200
But you had some very good
casting, Ben, for your father.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.634
And were you excited when this
person said they would do this?

00:05:51.634 --> 00:05:53.133
-I couldn't believe it.

00:05:53.133 --> 00:05:56.901
-I'm guessing it was like
your first thought was like,

00:05:56.901 --> 00:05:58.267
"What if this person
would say yes?"

00:05:58.267 --> 00:06:02.067
-Yeah, no, he was about 15th
on the list, and...

00:06:02.067 --> 00:06:04.300
Yeah. No, it was amazing.
I mean, do you want to show?

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.501
-It also kind of makes sense.

00:06:06.501 --> 00:06:09.033
I mean, if Conan O'Brien's going
to play any of your dads...

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:12.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:16.234
-Yeah. Yeah.
-He's very good.

00:06:16.234 --> 00:06:17.634
-Yeah.
-He's so good.

00:06:17.634 --> 00:06:21.334
And he's weirdly never played
a character in a movie

00:06:21.334 --> 00:06:23.133
that is not Conan O'Brien.
-Right.

00:06:23.133 --> 00:06:25.400
-So it was like
the first time he got to act.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.634
-Hopefully you guys
weren't judging that

00:06:27.634 --> 00:06:29.534
as you're still your own names.

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:30.934
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.934 --> 00:06:33.400
-"Oh, you've never
played a character?

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.667
What a loser. God."

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:39.167
-So, I mean, it must have been
so cool when he said yes.

00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:41.667
And you guys get to sort of
break him as an actor.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:42.968
I feel like that's
really thrilling.

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.634
-Yeah, it was fun to teach him
how show business works.

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:48.267
-Totally.
-No, it was amazing.

00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:51.734
I mean, he's such a legend.
We've all grown up on his stuff.

00:06:51.734 --> 00:06:53.601
So it was really cool.

00:06:53.601 --> 00:06:55.400
-The clip was
a very good example

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:56.968
of my favorite thing
in this movie

00:06:56.968 --> 00:06:58.634
is how lovingly you

00:06:58.634 --> 00:07:01.534
all just make fun
of each other constantly.

00:07:01.534 --> 00:07:03.100
This is a very lovely
through line

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:04.734
in a lot of your shorts as well.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.834
And I think it works
because we can tell you guys

00:07:06.834 --> 00:07:09.434
like each other.
When you write these jokes

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:13.067
where you're just like laying
into your closest friends,

00:07:13.067 --> 00:07:16.100
is it ever too close to the bone
or is it always just joy?

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.534
-It's...

00:07:17.534 --> 00:07:19.300
Yeah, sometimes it hurts. Um...

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.934
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:25.434
What about something about
how your head's really big?

00:07:25.434 --> 00:07:28.167
Maybe.

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:31.067
-We had something in one video
where we called Martin

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:33.767
like a melted
Harry Potter action figure.

00:07:33.767 --> 00:07:34.934
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.934 --> 00:07:38.100
-And that one
bordered on too mean.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.267
-Just being like...
[ Laughs awkwardly ]

00:07:40.267 --> 00:07:41.767
-I think most of the time,

00:07:41.767 --> 00:07:45.801
the relief that we have thought
of a joke surmounts anything.

00:07:45.801 --> 00:07:47.467
-Oh, right.

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:49.334
So you're like, you don't even
care that it's at your expense.

00:07:49.334 --> 00:07:50.868
You're like, "If this is
a laugh, we will take it."

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:53.334
-It works. Put it in. It's done.
-Yeah.

00:07:53.334 --> 00:07:56.033
"We'll talk about it
in 15 years in therapy.

00:07:56.033 --> 00:07:57.734
We do not have to deal
with this now."

00:07:57.734 --> 00:08:01.200
You guys also,
I'm just wondering,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.033
like when you're on set
and obviously, you know,

00:08:03.033 --> 00:08:04.734
Judd was probably on set
a fair amount of time.

00:08:04.734 --> 00:08:06.000
You got Conan on set.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.534
And it's this movie
you guys wrote.

00:08:07.534 --> 00:08:11.467
Was it surreal, like,
as you were shooting this thing?

00:08:11.467 --> 00:08:15.901
-Every day was --
Every day was a dream.

00:08:15.901 --> 00:08:18.367
I couldn't believe -- We grew up
on Judd's movies.

00:08:18.367 --> 00:08:22.033
And to be able to do one
with him was like mind-blowing.

00:08:22.033 --> 00:08:24.267
-Also, like 90% of our videos,

00:08:24.267 --> 00:08:27.334
we shoot in like a 10x10 office.
-Yeah.

00:08:27.334 --> 00:08:30.267
-And so we were like,
there's a scene in the movie

00:08:30.267 --> 00:08:34.434
where I'm being flown up
on a crane in a wing suit,

00:08:34.434 --> 00:08:35.801
like on a mountain,

00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:38.133
and it's just like the scale
was unbelievable.

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:40.667
-It was crazy.
And it was 102 degrees,

00:08:40.667 --> 00:08:44.100
like every day. We were in
North Carolina and it was --

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.334
Yeah, it was wild.
Yeah, it was really hard.

00:08:47.334 --> 00:08:49.467
-They say at 102, you want to be
in that wing suit.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:50.734
-Yeah.
-Yeah, definitely.

00:08:50.734 --> 00:08:54.601
-They had umbrellas
because the sun was too intense.

00:08:54.601 --> 00:08:56.200
-I had a guy come up to me
and said,

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.200
"The science says
if you take your shoes off,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.868
you'll cool down faster."
Like, it was like a wax suit

00:09:02.868 --> 00:09:05.334
that was like trapping
all the heat in.

00:09:05.334 --> 00:09:07.501
I was being raised
closer to the sun.

00:09:07.501 --> 00:09:10.701
Guys were like pouring
water bottles inside of me.

00:09:10.701 --> 00:09:12.801
I was like, "Let's do
another one. Let's go again.

00:09:12.801 --> 00:09:14.634
Ah!"

00:09:14.634 --> 00:09:17.334
-Whenever a guy is
like, "The science says"

00:09:17.334 --> 00:09:19.834
and it's like shoe-related,
it might not be a scientist.

00:09:19.834 --> 00:09:21.133
[ Laughter ]

00:09:21.133 --> 00:09:23.567
Yeah. You guys, you did
something very cool today,

00:09:23.567 --> 00:09:24.868
which I want
everybody here to know.

00:09:24.868 --> 00:09:27.701
You put the first four minutes
of the movie on YouTube,

00:09:27.701 --> 00:09:30.334
so right now you can
go check it out.

00:09:30.334 --> 00:09:32.267
It's very funny from the second
the movie starts.

00:09:32.267 --> 00:09:34.200
So I hope everybody will go
do that and check that out.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:35.968
-Thank you.
-Thank you so much.

00:09:35.968 --> 00:09:37.968
-Just one long YouTube
video basically.

00:09:37.968 --> 00:09:39.934
-Yeah.
-That's what movies are.

00:09:39.934 --> 00:09:42.767
-And then how are -- my last
thing before I let you guys go.

00:09:42.767 --> 00:09:43.968
How were you feeling
about this week?

00:09:43.968 --> 00:09:45.100
Are you excited
about "SNL" as well?

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:46.934
It must be a trippy week
to be promoting a movie,

00:09:46.934 --> 00:09:48.934
and also to know
that Lorne doesn't care

00:09:48.934 --> 00:09:50.634
and he wants you
to have a good Saturday.

00:09:50.634 --> 00:09:52.734
[ Laughter ]

00:09:52.734 --> 00:09:53.934
Lorne right now
is probably like,

00:09:53.934 --> 00:09:55.868
"Oh, are they promoting
their movie?"

00:09:55.868 --> 00:09:59.167
[ Laughter ]

00:09:59.167 --> 00:10:02.267
-That is the vibe.
-That's the vibe. Yeah, yeah.

00:10:02.267 --> 00:10:03.767
Guess what.
You're not imagining it.

00:10:03.767 --> 00:10:05.734
-No.
-No.

00:10:05.734 --> 00:10:07.501
-No, we did "The Tonight Show"
on Monday

00:10:07.501 --> 00:10:09.234
and he was like,
"You're late for pitch."

00:10:09.234 --> 00:10:10.667
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

